# The server's logs, shipped somewhere a person can read them.
#
# **Found by reading Sentry's weekly report and then checking what else was recording.**
# `describe-log-groups` returned nothing at all: the environment had `StreamLogs: false`, so
# every line gunicorn wrote went to a disk on one instance and was never read by anybody.
#
# **Sentry catches exceptions, and that is not the same thing as logs.** A 400 we return on
# purpose, a request that took forty seconds, a worker gunicorn killed on the timeout, a
# migration that printed a warning on deploy - none of those raise, so none of them reach
# Sentry, and until now none of them were written anywhere that survives. The hardening
# section of CLAUDE.md listed "logs and error monitoring" together as though both existed;
# only the second one did.
#
# **The day a beta tester says "it did not work" and there is no exception is exactly the day
# this is needed**, and it is also the day it is too late to turn on.
#
# It is here rather than applied with `update-environment` on purpose: an option set by hand
# is lost the moment the environment is rebuilt, and rebuilding is the normal answer to a
# broken environment - which is precisely when the logs matter.
option_settings:
  aws:elasticbeanstalk:cloudwatch:logs:
    StreamLogs: true

    # **Thirty days and not the default seven.** The window that matters is "a tester told me
    # something broke last week and I am only now looking" - seven days loses that routinely.
    # The volume is gunicorn's access log, which at this size is a few tens of megabytes a
    # month and costs cents; retention is the cheap half of CloudWatch, at about $0.03 per
    # gigabyte-month.
    RetentionInDays: 30

    # Keep them when the environment goes away. An environment is usually terminated *because*
    # something went wrong, and deleting the record of it in the same breath is the one
    # setting here that could destroy the thing it exists to provide.
    DeleteOnTerminate: false

# **Health event streaming stays off, and that is a decision.** It writes the environment's
# own health transitions, which `larson-api-down` already reports by email and which say
# nothing about what the application did. Turning it on would be a second stream of the fact
# we already have an alarm for - infrastructure for its own sake, which principle 1 forbids.
